{s:RC,k:'recap',term:'Recap',say:`Recap. An on-line ${P('UPS')} never switches; size it in volt-amps and usable watt-hours. A charger runs constant current, then constant voltage. And induction heating makes heat inside the metal, at a frequency for hot steel.`,cues:{r1:"An on-line",r2:"A charger runs",r3:"And induction heating"},panels:[["On-line UPS","BLU",'peUpsR',['Rate in VA; t = E / Pdc.','No transfer on a failure.'],'r1'],["CC-CV charger","ORG",'peChgR',['CC, then CV taper.','Ah in = Ah out / η.'],'r2'],["Induction heating","GRN",'peIndR',['Heat in the workpiece.','f chosen for hot steel.'],'r3']],live:[0,1,2]},
/* ---- exam practice ---- */
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 1',lv:'U',n:1,q:"Why does a CC-CV charger switch from CC to CV?",say:'Question one. Why does a charger switch from constant current to constant voltage?',cues:{k:'Question one',q:'Why does a'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 1',lv:'U',n:1,q:"Why does a CC-CV charger switch from CC to CV?",say:'Constant current pushes the charge in quickly. At the voltage limit, constant voltage holds it safe while the current tapers, so there is no overcharge.',cues:{a1:'Constant current pushes',a2:'At the voltage'},al:[["CC pushes the needed charge in quickly.",'a1'],["At the voltage limit, CV holds the safe voltage and the current tapers, so there is no overcharge.",'a2']],vis:(c,u,b)=>{const X=t=>50+t/7*530,YI=i=>440-i*26,YV=f=>150-f*80;return `
${PE_L(50,440,590,440,PE_AX,2)}${PE_L(50,60,50,440,PE_AX,2)}${PE_T(310,486,'time (h)',{fs:22,fw:700,a:'middle',fill:PE_MUT})}
${g('pop',tk(c,'a1'),PE_area([[X(0),440],[X(0),YI(10)],[X(PE_TCC),YI(10)],[X(PE_TCC),440]],BLU,.18)+PE_poly([[X(0),YI(10)],[X(PE_TCC),YI(10)]],BLU,5)+PE_T(X(PE_TCC/2),YI(10)+44,'CC: 10 A',{fs:26,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:BLU})+PE_poly([[X(0),YV(0)],[X(PE_TCC),YV(1)]],ORG,4)+PE_T(X(1),YV(.3)+30,'voltage rises',{fs:22,fw:800,fill:ORG})+PE_T(X(PE_TCC),466,'4.44',{fs:22,fill:PE_MUT,a:'middle'}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'a2'),PE_poly([[X(PE_TCC),YV(1)],[X(PE_TEND),YV(1)]],ORG,5)+PE_T(X(PE_TCC)+8,YV(1)-16,'CV: held at 14.4 V',{fs:24,fw:800,fill:ORG})+PE_L(X(PE_TCC),70,X(PE_TCC),440,PE_MUT,2,'stroke-dasharray="6 6"'))}
${g('grow',tk(c,['a2',2.4]),PE_area([[X(PE_TCC),440],[X(PE_TCC),YI(10)],[X(PE_TEND),YI(2)],[X(PE_TEND),440]],ORG,.18)+PE_poly([[X(PE_TCC),YI(10)],[X(PE_TEND),YI(2)]],BLU,5)+PE_T(X(PE_TEND)+8,YI(2)+8,'2 A',{fs:22,fw:800,fill:GRN})+PE_T(X(PE_TEND),466,'6.30',{fs:22,fill:PE_MUT,a:'middle'}))}`}},
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 2',lv:'A',n:2,q:"A 1500 VA UPS feeds a 900 W load at 0.75 power factor. Is the load within the VA rating?",say:`Question two. A fifteen hundred volt-amp ${P('UPS')} feeds nine hundred watts at zero point seven five power factor. Is it within rating?`,cues:{k:"Question two",q:"A fifteen hundred"},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 2',lv:'A',n:2,q:"A 1500 VA UPS feeds a 900 W load at 0.75 power factor. Is the load within the VA rating?",say:'Divide nine hundred by zero point seven five: twelve hundred volt-amps. That is below fifteen hundred, so the load fits, with three hundred volt-amps to spare.',cues:{fk:'Divide nine hundred',k1:'Divide nine hundred',k1b:'twelve hundred volt',k2:'That is below',k2b:'with three hundred'},calc:{f:['S = P / cos φ'],hint:'Compare VA with VA, not watts.',fk:'fk',steps:[["Load VA",["S = 900 / 0.75","S = 1200 VA"],["k1","k1b"]],["Compare",["1200 VA < 1500 VA","1200 VA: within rating, 300 VA margin"],["k2","k2b"]]],live:[0,1]}},
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 3',lv:'N',n:3,q:"Why can a 1000 VA UPS trip on a 600 W SMPS load?",say:`Question three. Why can a one thousand volt-amp ${P('UPS')} trip on a six hundred watt ${P('SMPS')} load?`,cues:{k:'Question three',q:'Why can a'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 3',lv:'N',n:3,q:"Why can a 1000 VA UPS trip on a 600 W SMPS load?",say:`The ${P('SMPS')} draws current in narrow peaks. Its high crest factor can push its ${P('RMS')} current past the sine-wave rating. The ${P('UPS')} must supply those peaks, so it may trip at low watts.`,cues:{a1:"draws current in",a2:"Its high crest",a3:"must supply those"},al:[["The SMPS draws current in narrow peaks.",'a1'],["Its crest factor is high, so its RMS current may exceed the sine-wave rating.",'a2'],["The UPS must supply the peaks; protection may trip even at low watts.",'a3']],vis:(c,u,b)=>{const X=t=>40+t/(2*Math.PI)*540,K=42,Yc=120,Y2=340;return `
${PE_T(310,28,'the same RMS current, one mains cycle',{fs:22,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:PE_MUT})}
${PE_L(40,Yc,580,Yc,PE_AX,2)}${PE_L(40,Y2,580,Y2,PE_AX,2)}
${PE_poly(PE_fn(t=>Math.SQRT2*Math.sin(t),0,2*Math.PI,X,v=>Yc-v*K),BLU,4)}${PE_T(580,52,'sine: crest 1.41',{fs:22,fw:800,a:'end',fill:BLU})}
${g('pop',tk(c,'a1'),PE_poly(PE_fn(t=>PE_CF*Math.sign(Math.sin(t))*Math.pow(Math.abs(Math.sin(t)),PE_N),0,2*Math.PI,X,v=>Y2-v*K,240),ORG,4)+PE_T(X(Math.PI/2)+40,Y2-PE_CF*K+40,'SMPS: narrow peaks',{fs:22,fw:800,fill:ORG}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'a2'),PE_L(40,Y2-PE_CF*K,580,Y2-PE_CF*K,ORG,2,'stroke-dasharray="6 6"')+PE_T(580,Y2-PE_CF*K-8,`crest ≈ ${PE_CF.toFixed(1)}`,{fs:24,fw:800,a:'end',fill:ORG}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'a3'),PE_box(40,400,280,50,{fill:'#FFF5F5',st:RED,sw:3,r:12})+PE_T(180,434,'peak current: trip',{fs:24,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:RED}))}`}},
{s:LS,k:'figs',term:'Your notes',title:'For your notes',say:`Your free notes are the PDF linked in the description. Redraw the ${P('UPS')}, the charging curve and the induction heater.`,cues:{t:"Your free notes",f:"Redraw the"},tags:[["Free notes for this lesson (PDF)", "link in the description"], ["In this lesson", "Uninterruptible power supplies; battery chargers and emergency lighting; induction heating"], ["Key terms and glossary", "at the end of the notes"]],figs:[["The on-line UPS", "Mains, rectifier and charger, a 48 V DC bus with the battery, the inverter and the load; static and maintenance bypasses on top."], ["The CC-CV charge", "10 A for 4.44 h returns 44.4 Ah; held at 14.4 V, the current tapers to 2 A and adds 11.1 Ah."], ["The induction heater", "DC link, full-bridge inverter at 2 kHz, tank capacitor and work coil; the billet's current flows one skin depth deep."]],note:"Sources your free notes draw on: Mohan, Undeland and Robbins (Power Electronics: Converters, Applications and Design); Rashid (Power Electronics: Devices, Circuits, and Applications); Bimbhra (Power Electronics)"},
{s:OUT,k:'outro',term:'Outro',say:`So remember: on-line means no transfer, a charger pushes in more than came out, and induction heats the skin. Next time, ${P('HVDC')} transmission. See you there!`,cues:{f:'So remember',w1:'on-line means no',w2:'a charger pushes',w3:'and induction heats',n:'Next time',s:'See you there'},words:[['ON-LINE: NO TRANSFER','BLU','w1'],['Ah IN = Ah OUT / η','ORG','w2'],['HEAT IN THE SKIN δ','GRN','w3']],next:["NEXT TIME", "Next lesson", "HVDC transmission · Static VAR compensators and FACTS", "book"],end:'See you there!'}
